T20:29:18Z","updated_at":"2026-10-20T18:39:31Z","due_on":null,"closed_at":"2026-06-19T16:54:35Z"},"comments":4,"created_at":"2026-01-10T08:25:27Z","updated_at":"2026-01-26T21:06:29Z","closed_at":"2026-01-26T21:04:57Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproduceable in: 4.5.1, 4.4.1, 4.6-dev3\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.5.1.stable - macOS Tahoe (26.1.0) - Multi-window, 1 monitor - OpenGL 3 (Compatibility) - Apple M4 Pro - Apple M4 Pro (14 threads) - 24.00 GiB memory\n\n### Issue description\n\nI have an Xbox Series X controller that I'm testing on macOS Tahoe (26.1)\n\nWhen connected via Bluetooth, the controller works just fine and is detected as:\n```\nXbox Series X Controller\n```\nIt will vibrate when told to.\n\nHowever, when connected directly to my Macbook Pro via a USB-C to USB-C cable, it is simply detected as:\n```\nController\n```\nAnd vibration does not work. As far as I can tell, all other functionality works.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n- Plug Xbox Series X controller directly into Macbook Pro\n- It is detected as `Controller` by `Input.get_joy_name(0)`\n- Try to vibrate it with `Input.start_joy_vibration(0, 0.5, 0.5, 0.2)`\n- No vibration happens, however input is still detected\n- Try same controller over Bluetooth\n- Works fine\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nhttps://github.com/TranquilMarmot/godot-xbox-controller-macos-detection\n\nSimple project with just a button to vibrate the controller","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo","id":17489292,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE3NDg5Mjky","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/17489292?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Repiteo","html_url":"https://github.com/Repiteo","followers_url":"https: